---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
---

<Page
  title="Methodology"
  description="How SoftenerIndex handles hardness, installer lists, and prices: sourced frontmatter or blank fields. Never invented numbers."
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap prose">
      <p class="kicker">Editorial standards</p>
      <h1>Methodology</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        SoftenerIndex would rather ship an empty data box than a round number that sounds
        local. This page is the public version of the nightly frontmatter workflow in the
        repository README.
      </p>
      <h2>What may appear as a number</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Hardness</strong> only with <code>hardnessSource</code> (name, URL, retrieved
          date). Grains per gallon and mg/L as CaCO₃ are optional fields; the schema refuses
          either without a source.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Installers</strong> only as rows with a name and a source. City pages stay
          <code>listingStatus: research</code> until that array is non-empty.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Prices</strong> are not a collection field. Guides talk about quote structure,
          not national or Phoenix averages we did not collect.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h2>What will not appear</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Converted adjectives (“very hard”) turned into grains.</li>
        <li>Dealer counts inferred from ads or map pins.</li>
        <li>Installed-cost ranges copied from other content farms.</li>
        <li>Performance percentages for salt-free devices we did not test.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>City pages</h2>
      <p>
        Phoenix ships first, linked to City of Phoenix water-quality materials and the Arizona
        Registrar of Contractors. Those links are starting points for the reader. They are not
        a substitute for pasting a hardness table into frontmatter without reading it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each city file has <code>lastVerified</code>. The nightly workflow updates that date
        even when nothing new is sourced, so “we looked” is visible and “we guessed” is not.
      </p>
      <h2>Corrections</h2>
      <p>
        If a sourced figure changes in a later utility report, replace the number, the URL if
        needed, and the retrieved date in the same commit. Do not leave a stale grain value
        with a fresh timestamp.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Page>
